Post-COVID-19 Condition (Long COVID)
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Profound persisting fatigue >3 months post-COVID, Post-exertional malaise, Severe brain fog/cognitive impairment, Persistent anosmia/parosmia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-036
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe palpitations, chest tightness, and profound orthostatic dizziness requiring urgent assessment to rule out myocarditis or cardiopulmonary decompensation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing sudden exertional tachycardia, persistent chest tightness, and presyncope upon standing. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate cardiovascular stability, rule out acute arrhythmias or pulmonary embolism, and ensure safety prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I had COVID four months ago, but today my heart is racing so fast I feel dizzy, my chest is tight, and my brain is completely blank."
History of Present Illness: The initial acute COVID-19 infection resolved four months ago. However, the patient reports profound, newly onset, and unrelenting generalized fatigue persisting strictly since the infection. The timeline demonstrates a chronic state of post-exertional malaise (PEM), characterized by a severe, disproportionate crash following minor physical exertion. Over the past 24 hours, the patient has developed sudden exertional tachycardia, severe palpitations, and profound orthostatic intolerance upon standing. The patient also notes severe cognitive impairment, describing it as 'brain fog', causing profound difficulty with concentration, alongside unresolving anosmia.
Functional Status: Bedbound, actively avoiding standing due to extreme dizziness and tachycardia.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding the sudden cardiac symptoms; spouse is highly distressed by the cognitive decline.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Fatigued, chronically ill-appearing, visibly anxious, gripping the bedrails when attempting to sit up.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a regular but rapid rate; no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally with no wheeze or crackles. Neurological exam reveals an alert patient with delayed verbal processing. Orthostatic vital signs demonstrate a significant heart rate jump (from 80 supine to 110 standing) without severe hypotension. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Profound persisting fatigue >3 months post-COVID, Post-exertional malaise, Severe brain fog/cognitive impairment, and Persistent anosmia/parosmia.
Environmental Observations: Room is darkened; patient has multiple half-empty water glasses at bedside.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Unremarkable recent medical history prior to COVID-19.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-COVID-19 Condition (Long COVID) with severe dysautonomia - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Myocarditis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of a new pathological heart murmur, friction rub, or signs of acute heart failure (e.g., elevated JVP, peripheral edema).

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal oxygen saturations on room air, clear lung fields, and absence of unilateral calf swelling or acute pleuritic pain.

Generalized Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct, measurable orthostatic tachycardia and the clear chronological link to a documented viral infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute exacerbation of post-viral dysautonomia (POTS-like presentation) characterized by severe orthostatic tachycardia and chest tightness. The presence of sudden cardiac symptoms and presyncope necessitated an immediate, in-person clinical examination to confidently rule out life-threatening structural cardiopulmonary emergencies such as pulmonary embolism or late-onset viral myocarditis. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period carried a substantial risk of missing an acute cardiac event or allowing the patient to sustain a syncopal fall, thus strictly fulfilling the criteria for urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advise immediate supine rest. Prescribe strict fluid and sodium loading protocols. Urgent referral for a comprehensive in-hours medical evaluation including a 12-lead ECG, Holter monitor, echocardiogram, and basic lung function tests to formally exclude structural end-organ damage.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly implementing an activity pacing strategy and stopping a task the moment mild fatigue sets in, the patient will prevent the severe, debilitating post-exertional crash; avoiding this crash will positively reinforce the difficult habit of radical rest and energy conservation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized Long COVID rehabilitation clinic.
DME/Safety (Optional): Shower chair to prevent syncopal falls.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours for ECG and to initiate structured pacing plans.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the patient develops crushing central chest pain, severe shortness of breath at rest, a sudden loss of consciousness (fainting), or a resting heart rate consistently above 130 bpm that does not improve when lying flat.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; structural cardiac damage must still be formally excluded by specialized testing.
Clinical Course: This condition represents a prolonged, dysfunctional nervous system response following the virus. Recovery is strictly non-linear and requires aggressive rest and pacing to allow the autonomic system to reset.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your autonomic nervous system is currently misfiring, causing your heart to race inappropriately. If you experience severe, heavy chest pain or if you actually pass out, you must call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The virus has cleared, but it left your nervous system hypersensitive. The racing heart when you stand is because your blood vessels are not constricting properly to push blood to your brain. Drinking high volumes of water with added salt increases your blood volume, making it easier for your heart to maintain blood pressure without racing.
Actionable Steps: Drink 500mL of water before getting out of bed in the morning. Sit on the edge of the bed for 2 full minutes before standing. Never push through the fatigue.

